      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to study for my signals class and im totally stuck on the fourier transform. i get the math formulas but i dont really get the actual intuition. like, what is it actually doing to a signal? can u explain it simply?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Fourier Transform, in simple terms, is like a magical lens that lets you see a signal not just as a wiggly line over time, but as a collection of pure, underlying frequencies. It tells you *which* specific musical notes (frequencies) are present in your signal and *how loud* each note is. Think of it as taking a complex song and breaking it down into its individual constituent notes. This is incredibly useful for analyzing noise, filtering, and understanding how signals behave.",
